# Overview
#
# Each word rates a part as important without saying what depends on it
# (`the retry is crucial`). The reader learns that the part matters, but
# not what breaks without it. The fix says what depends on the part
# (`without the retry, a lease error fails the job`), or deletes the
# word. The rule sits at Recommended, because `essential` also names a
# class of things.
#
# Expression
#
# `critical` counts only before `to` or `for` (`the lock is critical to
# the sweep`). `essential` skips a hyphen before it. Weir has no
# lookbehind, so the filter consumes the token before `essential` and
# narrows the finding back to `essential`.
#
# Two branches match a longer phrase. `plays a ... role` counts with
# `vital`, `crucial`, `key`, `critical`, or `important` in the gap, and
# `underscore` or `highlight`, with or without a final `s`, counts
# before `the importance` or `the need`.
#
# False Positives
#
# `critical` alone names a critical section, so it passes.
# `non-essential` names a class of request rather than rating one part,
# so it passes too.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 65 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits rated a part without saying what depends on it. Both misfires
# used `essential` for a class (`any essential settings`), the way
# `non-essential` does.
expr main [crucial, <(!-essential), essential>, (critical [to, for]), cornerstone, linchpin, vital, pivotal, paramount, invaluable, indispensable, (plays a [vital, crucial, key, critical, important] role), ([underscore, underscores, highlight, highlights] the [importance, need])]
let message "Importance claim. Say what depends on it, or delete the word."
let description "Flags words that rate a part as important without saying what depends on it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The mutex guards the critical section."
allows "The key role field names the primary role."
allows "Isolate non-essential requests."
